'''beneficently''' (bē-nef′i-sẹnt-li), ''adv.'' In a beneficent manner.

'''beneficia''', ''n.'' Plural of ''beneficium.''

'''beneficial''' (ben-ē-fish′a̤l), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< LL. ''beneficialis'', < L. ''beneficium'', a benefit: see ''benefice.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Contributing to a valuable end; conferring benefit; advantageous; profitable; useful; helpful.
{{smaller block|The war which would have been most ''beneficial'' to us.
{{right|''Swift.''}}
That which is ''beneficial'' to the community as a whole, it will become the private interest of some part of the community to accomplish. ''H. Spencer'', Social Statics, p. 443.}}
'''2.''' Having or conferring the right to the use or benefit, as of property; pertaining or entitled to the usufruct: as, a ''beneficial'' owner (which see, below); a ''beneficial'' interest in an estate.—'''3†.''' Pertaining to or having a benefice; beneficed.
{{smaller block|An engagement was tendered to all civil officers and ''beneficial'' clergy.
{{right|''Hallam.''}}}}
'''4†.''' Kind; generous: as, a “''beneficial'' foe,” ''B. Jonson.''—'''Beneficial owner''', {{smaller|one who, though not having apparent legal title, is in equity entitled to enjoy the advantage of ownership.}}='''Syn. 1.''' {{smaller|''Beneficent'', ''Beneficial'' (see ''beneficent''), good, salutary.}}

'''II.†''' ''n.'' A benefice; a church living.
{{smaller block|<poem>For that the ground-worke is, and end of all,
How to obtaine a ''Beneficiall.''</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', Mother Hub. Tale, l. 486.}}}}

